
  [1mThe fork: where does [38;5;153muserId[39m come from?[0m The login form collects only username and password ([38;5;153mindex.html:8[39m), and [38;5;153mlogin[39m is
  a stub that doesn't call the API yet. Nothing in the app currently holds a user ID, so a new parameter has no value
  to receive. How you want that resolved changes the shape of the work:

  - [1mCaller supplies it[0m — add [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m and thread a value in from somewhere new (a hidden field,
    a stored value, a generated client ID). Buys you exactly what you asked for. Costs: something has to produce the ID
    before login runs, and if that source doesn't exist yet, we're building it. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mServer returns it[0m — leave the signature alone; the real API returns a user ID on success and [38;5;153mlogin[39m surfaces it in
    its return value for tracking. Buys you an ID that actually identifies the account, since the server is the only
    party that knows it. Costs: nothing to track until the stub is replaced with a real call. This is the conventional
    shape for auth.
  - [1mIt's a correlation ID, not an identity[0m — add an optional trailing [38;5;153muserId[39m used to tie log lines together across a
    session. Buys traceability now with no API dependency. Costs: it doesn't tell you [3mwho[0m, only that requests belong
    together.

  My recommendation is the second: "track who logged in" is identity, and in an auth flow the client can't know the
  user's ID before authenticating — passing one in means trusting the client to assert who it is. If the intent is
  really the third option, the name [38;5;153muserId[39m will mislead whoever reads it next.

  I also don't know what "track" should do. Right now it's a [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m. If you want tracking to go anywhere real (an
  analytics call, a log store), that's a new piece of the app and I'd want to design it rather than bolt it on — tell me
  and I'll step up to a fuller design.
